📘 Knowledge Graph – Structured Explanation (Loan Approval Example)
🔹 1. What is a Knowledge Graph?
A Knowledge Graph is a way of organizing data as a network of entities (things) and relationships between them.
Unlike traditional databases that use tables, a knowledge graph represents data using:
Nodes (things)
Relationships (connections)
Properties (details)
Example:
A bank analyzing loan applications:
Customer → applies_for → Loan
👉 This shows how a customer is connected to a loan directly.
🔹 2. Core Components of Knowledge Graph
2.1 Nodes (Entities)
Nodes represent real-world objects.
Examples (Loan Domain):
Customer
Loan
Bank
Credit Score
Example Representation:
(Customer: Ravi)
(Loan: Home Loan)
👉 These are the main objects in the system.
2.2 Relationships (Edges)
Relationships connect nodes and are always directional.
Example:
(Customer: Ravi) → APPLIES_FOR → (Loan: Home Loan)
👉 Meaning: Ravi has applied for a home loan.
Another example:
(Customer: Ravi) → HAS_CREDIT_SCORE → (Score: 750)
👉 Helps in decision-making.
2.3 Properties
Properties are attributes of nodes or relationships.
Example:
(Customer {
 name: "Ravi",
 age: 28,
 income: 50000
})
(Loan {
 type: "Home Loan",
 amount: 2000000
})
👉 These details help determine loan eligibility.
2.4 Labels / Types
Labels define the category of a node.
Example:
(:Customer)
(:Loan)
(:Bank)
👉 Helps classify data clearly.
2.5 Triples (Important Concept)
A knowledge graph is often represented as:
Subject → Predicate → Object
Example:
Ravi → applies_for → Home Loan
👉 This is the basic building block of a knowledge graph.
🔹 3. Node–Relationship Structure
The general structure of a graph:
(Node) --[RELATIONSHIP]--> (Node)
Real Example:
(Customer:Ravi) --[APPLIES_FOR]--> (Loan:Home Loan)
(Customer:Ravi) --[HAS_INCOME]--> (Income:50000)
👉 This structure helps banks easily analyze connections.
