🔹 5. Example Comparison
5.1 SQL (Relational Model)
Tables:
Customer(id, name)
Loan(id, type)
Application(customer_id, loan_id)
Query:
SQL
SELECT c.name, l.type  
FROM Customer c  
JOIN Application a ON c.id = a.customer_id  
JOIN Loan l ON a.loan_id = l.id;
👉 Requires multiple joins to fetch data.
5.2 Knowledge Graph Model
(Customer:Ravi) → APPLIES_FOR → (Loan:Home Loan)
👉 No joins required; relationship is directly stored.
🔹 6. Query Languages in Knowledge Graph
Common query languages include:
SPARQL → Used for RDF graphs
Cypher → Used in Neo4j
Gremlin → Used in Apache TinkerPop
🔹 7. Neo4j and Cypher
Neo4j is a popular graph database that uses the Cypher query language to interact with data.
🔹 8. Cypher Basics
8.1 Create Nodes
Cypher
CREATE (c:Customer {name: "Ravi"})
8.2 Create Relationship
Cypher
CREATE (c:Customer {name:"Ravi"})
CREATE (l:Loan {type:"Home Loan"})
CREATE (c)-[:APPLIES_FOR]->(l)
8.3 Match (Query Data)
Cypher
MATCH (c:Customer)-[:APPLIES_FOR]->(l)
RETURN c, l
👉 Fetch all customers and their loans.
8.4 Filtering
Cypher
MATCH (c:Customer {name:"Ravi"})
RETURN c
👉 Fetch specific customer data.
8.5 Pattern Matching
Cypher
MATCH (c)-[:APPLIES_FOR]->(l)-[:APPROVED_BY]->(b)
RETURN c, l, b
👉 Finds which bank approved which loan.
